/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (TF had already developed substantial internal resources, including infrastructure vulnerability data and repair cost assumptions across asset classes. In particular, their internal library contained a detailed methodological document outlining how climate hazards are translated into physical damage and GDP-related losses.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (However, the application of this methodology to a new country had not yet been completed and required significant upfront work: compiling and validating region-specific datasets, adapting network representations, and ensuring that vulnerability assumptions remain credible in different physical and institutional contexts. As TF is still a relatively new organisation, much of this work was ongoing. The underlying frameworks, data, and plans existed, but had not yet been fully operationalised into reproducible, end-to-end outputs.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (In the initial project brief, TF indicated that our work could involve either model prototyping with full documentation of assumptions and code, or structured research and reporting. Beyond this, the scope was intentionally open-ended. One of the first steps of the project therefore consisted of clarifying objectives jointly with the client. Early discussions revealed that TF was preparing for prospective client engagements in India and Uganda, but had not yet completed their goals for these countries.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
