267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 139 0 R
/T (Simplified Flows of Electricity)
/E (Simplified Flows of Electricity)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (Due to the laws of electricity and physics, transmission lines do not have unlimited carrying capacity and lose electricity over time due to resistance \(Britannica\). The model does not capture either phenomenon. Transmission lines are treated as having unlimited capacity, and losses during transmission are ignored. As a result, power flows in the model do not strictly obey the laws of electricity and should be understood as simplified estimates rather than physically realistic simulations. The lack of a capacity constraint per transmission line is the more fundamental issue, as it affects how electricity is routed and rerouted through the network following a climate hazard. Resistance losses are less important by comparison, as they primarily inflate quantities rather than change flow structure.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (Relevance: This makes the model unsuitable for operational grid planning or load-balancing analysis. However, for  climate risk modelling, where relative exposure and connectivity matter more than exact physical behaviour, this simplification is acceptable. Our contact at TF assured us that the model did not require such realism, as it would add unnecessary complexity and computational load.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
