299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (           The most significant limitation concerns the representation of electricity flows. However, the allocation approach adopted mirrors that used in established professional projects such as Open GIRA, where precise electrical behaviour is secondary to modelling the structure of the network, the location of generation assets, and the distribution of demand. Other potential concerns, such as data granularity, were discussed with the client and deemed acceptable. As emphasised by TF, the climate-hazard risk model is intended as a tool for understanding and comparing risk, not as a fully realistic simulation of power system operations.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (       As a result, the project reduced development time for TF and demonstrated that their methodology can be successfully translated to new regional contexts. The work also helped surface the practical challenges involved in extending the model to a country as large and complex as India. With the electricity grid layer now rebuilt, TF is able to proceed with integrating it into the wider modelling pipeline and addressing outstanding issues related to visualisation and downstream analysis.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
