314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (A key conceptual distinction underlying this work is the separation between hazard, vulnerability, and loss. A hazard describes the physical intensity of a climate stressor; vulnerability captures how this hazard translates into physical damage and performance degradation, conditional on asset characteristics; in this case, increasing flood depth may raise the likelihood of damage to turbines, electrical equipment, or auxiliary infrastructure. Loss then refers to the resulting quantified reduction in electricity generation and associated revenue losses. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
