/T (However, we know that sedimentation does not occur linearly but is driven by flood events which mobilise sediment and carry it to reservoirs.  To distribute the annual predicted capacity loss across different flood magnitudes a function was created to assess sediment mobilisation per flood event yielding the following results: )
/E (However, we know that sedimentation does not occur linearly but is driven by flood events which mobilise sediment and carry it to reservoirs.  To distribute the annual predicted capacity loss across different flood magnitudes a function was created to assess sediment mobilisation per flood event yielding the following results: )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 148 0 R
/T (These values represent the per-event capacity loss for a 1-in-5,10, 25 and 50 year floods. The values are small yet significant, specifically for floods of higher magnitudes.  Overall, our results suggest a significant sedimentation risk that will likely need significant maintenance and/ or adaptation measures to ensure electricity production and limit sedimentation impact from high intensity floods which may become more frequent due to various climate change factors.)
/E (These values represent the per-event capacity loss for a 1-in-5,10, 25 and 50 year floods. The values are small yet significant, specifically for floods of higher magnitudes.  Overall, our results suggest a significant sedimentation risk that will likely need significant maintenance and/ or adaptation measures to ensure electricity production and limit sedimentation impact from high intensity floods which may become more frequent due to various climate change factors.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 148 0 R
/T (Expected annual dam storage losses \(0.63% \(ICOLD, 2009\)\) were accumulated over time to estimate cumulative storage degradation. This yields projected capacity reductions of 3.15% over 5 years, 6.30% over 10 years, 12.59% over 20 years, and 31.48% over 50 years. This indicates significant long-term sedimentation risk to storage capacity for the Kikagati hydropower plant. Maintenance will be required and additional costs to cover this must be taken into account. )
/E (Expected annual dam storage losses \(0.63% \(ICOLD, 2009\)\) were accumulated over time to estimate cumulative storage degradation. This yields projected capacity reductions of 3.15% over 5 years, 6.30% over 10 years, 12.59% over 20 years, and 31.48% over 50 years. This indicates significant long-term sedimentation risk to storage capacity for the Kikagati hydropower plant. Maintenance will be required and additional costs to cover this must be taken into account. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
