/E (The analysis therefore focused on generation impacts inferred from physical exposure, using hazard intensity metrics combined with customised vulnerability functions rather than calibration to observed plant performance. Hence, results capture relative vulnerability across assets and hazards, but may not fully reflect plant-specific operational flexibility or short-term adaptive responses.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
373 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 780 0 R
/E (Absence of region- and asset-specific vulnerability curves: No empirically derived vulnerability curves exist for hydropower assets in Uganda, or for comparable assets in similar hydrological and climatic contexts. In particular, there is limited empirical evidence linking drought severity, flood depth, or heat stress directly to generation loss at the asset level.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 781 0 R
/E (Vulnerability functions were constructed using: asset typology \(run-of-river vs. reservoir-based\), engineering intuition regarding buffering and storage effects \(research-backed\), functional forms commonly applied in infrastructure risk modelling \(e.g. WRI Aqueduct, OS-Climate Physrisk, IMIA guidance\). Hence, these functions provide a conceptually sound but simplified representation of vulnerability. Results should therefore be interpreted as indicative of sensitivity, not precise forecasts of realised losses.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
